D.  Photoionization vs Compton on neutral hydrogen
==============================================================================
   sigma_pi: Karzas & Latter (1961) H(1s), threshold value
             6.3e-22 m^2 at 13.6057 eV
   sigma_C : (1+x_e) sigma_KN per H nucleus, x_e = 1e-04

   sigma_pi = sigma_C  at  E = 2333 eV = 2.333 keV

   E [eV]       sigma_pi [m^2] sigma_C [m^2]  ratio pi/C
   13.7         6.18498e-22    6.65277e-29    9.297e+06
   20           2.21051e-22    6.65260e-29    3.323e+06
   50           1.58270e-23    6.65182e-29    2.379e+05
   100          1.93378e-24    6.65052e-29    2.908e+04
   300          5.98152e-26    6.64532e-29    900.1
   1000         1.14075e-27    6.62722e-29    17.21
   2000         1.11006e-28    6.60157e-29    1.682
   2333         6.59309e-29    6.59309e-29    1
   5000         4.90787e-30    6.52616e-29    0.0752
   1e+04        4.54044e-31    6.40534e-29    0.007089
   1e+05        1.55080e-34    4.92798e-29    3.147e-06

   Maximum Compton recoil T_max = E 2a/(1+2a) reaches B_H = 13.6057 eV
   only at E = 1871 eV = 1.87 keV.
   Below that energy every Compton event is sub-threshold: the model
   gives no ionization and returns the photon with E' ~ E, which is
   what bound-electron (Rayleigh / Raman) scattering does as well, so
   the impulse approximation cannot bias the ionization budget there.
   Above it, T >> B_H within a factor of a few and the impulse
   approximation is the standard one (e.g. Zdziarski & Svensson 1989).
   Photoionization still dominates the interaction rate up to
   2333 eV, so Compton never controls the yield below ~2 keV.

